{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000cb11","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b0c8-2197-71f2-b37c-575c8ab978c8"},"sequence":995,"recorded_at":1780531400001985,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"53d7abd4-2111-4f48-89d8-7ded249d4ac3","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"53d7abd4-2111-4f48-89d8-7ded249d4ac3","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"53d7abd4-2111-4f48-89d8-7ded249d4ac3"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b0cc-1d89-7bb0-af24-a6f4edfe25e2"},"task_id":"01a0b0cc-1d89-7bb0-af24-a6f4edfe25e2","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b0cc-1d89-7bb0-af24-a6f4edfe25e2","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-22-1\",\n  \"command\": \"grep -n \\\"bootstrap window\\\\|first checkpoint\\\\|HasCheckpoint\\\\|13\\\\.1\\\" internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md | head -n 60\\necho \\\"===4.1 bootstrap section===\\\"\\nsed -n '1350,1420p' internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md\\necho \\\"===after-restore===\\\"\\ngrep -n \\\"after-restore\\\\|After-restore\\\\|step 5\\\\|Step 5\\\" internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md | head -n 30\",\n  \"description\": \"Read bootstrap window spec\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"740:13.13 adds a conformance gate over those flows, not a new component or sequence\\n1196:a changed operation in the bootstrap window is <code>idempotency_mismatch</code>.\\n1378:the bootstrap window is <code>idempotency_mismatch</code>. After a lost result,\\n1734:13.14.5 and 13.15 define the independently closed <code>2.0.0</code> and\\n2336:authoritative <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> event, and the Section 13.1\\n6301:Sections 5, 7, 10, 12, and 13.14.\\n6340:Environment Observation reuses the Section 13.14 Environment Tuple admission\\n8993:### 13.1 Direct-session launch\\n9044:<em>bootstrap window</em>. The epoch-1 lease is authoritative with\\n9058:| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-CHECKPOINT</code> | Captured objects may exist but no Checkpoint Record is authoritative | Validate/reuse immutable objects and publish the first checkpoint, or abort; objects are never relabeled as a checkpoint |\\n9110:   Section 13.1, then create and durably persist the\\n9153:The task-board launch remains in the same bootstrap window until step 7. The\\n9161:| <code>BOOT-TB-AFTER-EXPORT</code> | Reconcile the same export operation, validate bundle/proof, and publish the first checkpoint |\\n9166:the exact epoch-1/null-checkpoint/no-abort predicate in Section 13.1.\\n9842:publishing the exact two events in Section 13.1. A force stop of a session that\\n9852:### 13.10 Resume\\n9859:resume: it executes only the Section 13.1/13.2 bootstrap retry predicate and\\n9918:### 13.11 Reboot restore\\n9943:### 13.12 Failure and recovery matrix\\n9955:| Epoch-1 launch fails before first checkpoint | Never report stopped/resumable or invent a checkpoint | Reconcile and retry the same bootstrap identity, establish the first checkpoint, or prove closure and record non-resumable bootstrap abort |\\n9967:### 13.13 Crash/restart outcome gate\\n10003:| <code>CR-LAUNCH-D-01..05</code> | Direct bootstrap: after session/lease/event persistence; terminal creation; provider process start; Provider Identity persistence; capture/object validation, each before the next Section 13.1 phase or first-checkpoint publication. |\\n10013:| <code>CR-RESUME-01..06</code> | Owner resume: after owner/checkpoint/profile validation; prepare; prepared commit; exact native discovery or dormant manager reconciliation; provider/bridge activation may have occurred; finalize may have occurred before exactly-once <code>session.resumed</code>, each before the next Section 13.10 phase. |\\n10014:| <code>CR-RESTORE-01..07</code> | Reboot restore: after index validation; prior-live-session enumeration; lease refresh; stale-owner marking; checkpoint/profile validation; wrapper recreation; auto-resume may have occurred before durable status/event reconciliation, each before the next Section 13.11 phase. |\\n10037:and G4. Every prefix selects exactly one Section 13.13 outcome and preserves\\n10041:### 13.14 Cross-environment clone\\n10050:#### 13.14.1 Capture and canonical contracts\\n10267:#### 13.14.2 Fidelity, projection, and lineage\\n10561:#### 13.14.3 Immutable bundle chain\\n10623:#### 13.14.4 Transaction and target Checkpoint\\n10873:#### 13.14.5 Events, state, and tuple admission\\n10985:### 13.15 Directory continuation planning and execution\\n11014:| Managed or unmanaged / clone | different environment | <code>cross_environment_clone</code>; Section 13.14 plus AX transfer/launch |\\n11047:Section 13.14. Directory receipts reference those effect receipts and never\\n12358:with the local host UUID. Before the first checkpoint, both newest-checkpoint\\n12400:The crash gap inside Section 13.1 step 2 (Session Record durable, initial Lease\\n12427:record-only case. Normal Section 13.1 bootstrap retry begins only after these\\n15663:signed Environment Tuple under Section 13.14.5.\\n15697:| <code>AC-BOOT-001</code> | Every epoch-1 pre-checkpoint direct/task-board launch failure is either idempotently retried under the same process/manager identity, promoted through a first checkpoint, or force-closed as a null-checkpoint non-resumable failed bootstrap; RPC/Event/CLI shapes agree. |\\n15710:| <code>AC-CRASH-001</code> | Every applicable <code>CR-LAUNCH-*</code>, <code>CR-SYNC-*</code>, <code>CR-MAT-*</code>, <code>CR-GRACE-*</code>, <code>CR-FORCE-*</code>, <code>CR-FORK-*</code>, <code>CR-STOP-*</code>, <code>CR-RESUME-*</code>, and <code>CR-RESTORE-*</code> injection classifies into exactly one of <code>safe_retry</code>, <code>explicit_rollback</code>, or <code>recoverable_parked_state</code> with the Section 13.13 evidence record; no run produces duplicate live/authoritative owners, treats an unfenced external continuation as safe, or substitutes a fresh native provider/manager session for the persisted identity. |\\n15715:| <code>AC-CLONE-005</code> | Every <code>CR-CLONE-01..16</code> boundary selects exactly one Section 13.13 outcome; rollback remains available through finalization intent and is forbidden after Provider commit; all bundle evidence survives. |\\n15881:Section 13.13 outcome vocabulary is mutually exclusive and collectively\\n15911:| Terminal persistence | Sections 3.1–3.2, 4, 13.4–13.13, 19.2–19.4 |\\n15912:| Providers and native stores | Sections 7, 8, 13.1, 13.6–13.13, Appendix C |\\n15913:| Task-board integration | Sections 2.2, 9, 13.2, 13.6–13.13, 19.3–19.4 |\\n15915:| Mesh and replication | Sections 1.6, 3.2–3.3, 6, 10–12, 13.3, 13.13, 16, 19 |\\n15927:| macOS, Linux, WSL2, and native Windows PowerShell are explicit | Sections 3.2, 4, 8.4, 11.6, 13.11, and 19.2–19.3 |\\n15942:| Direct native-store and opaque task-board paths are distinct | Sections 2.1–2.2, 7–9, 13.1–13.2 |\\n15950:| Crash/restart classification has one exhaustive outcome and preserves owner/native identity | Sections 13.13, 19.4 <code>AC-CRASH-001</code>, 20.2 <code>SPEC-PUB-CRASH-001</code>, and Appendix A.8 |\\n15951:| Approved v0.4.3 roadmap and macOS terminal-realm boundaries | Sections 2.2–2.3, 3.1–3.2, 4.2/4.4, 7.1, 12.1–12.3, 13.3/13.5–13.6/13.11/13.15, 14.1, 15.3, 16.2, 17.1, 19.1–19.4, and Appendix D |\\n15974:| Workspace Group membership had no evolution rule | Immutable topology with membership derived from immutable Session Records and authoritative session Tombstones; exact expectation, concurrent join/leave, and group/worktree takeover rules in Sections 5.6, 11.3, 12.6, and 13.1–13.7 |\\n15983:| Materialization could not represent passive/stopped or non-workspace work | Section 11.3 defines MaterializationKind/Intent/Cohort/Sources and tagged request/result nullability; Sections 13.3, 13.6–13.7, and 13.10 select passive replica, owner resume, or ownership transfer explicitly; <code>AC-MAT-002</code> covers all six lanes |\\n15993:| Task-board materialization lacked durable subtransaction recovery | Section 10.6 defines the exact journaled bridge IDs, tokens, references, state/null rules, order, cleanup, and crash/expiry cases; Section 11.3 exposes token-free status and Sections 13.3/13.6–13.8/13.10 use the same coordinator; <code>AC-TB-004</code> covers all paths |\\n15995:| Owner resume omitted commit/finalize/rollback | Section 13.10 defines no-materialization, direct, and task-board paths through prepare, commit/prepared, activation, finalize or phase-safe rollback/status recovery; <code>AC-RESUME-001</code> covers every source-kind lane |\\n15997:| Epoch-1 failed launch had no stop/resume representation | Sections 5.2, 5.7, 11.3, 13.1–13.2, 13.9–13.10, and 14.2 define bootstrap retry/abort, nullable checkpoint, non-resumable failed state, and exact RPC/Event/CLI agreement; <code>AC-BOOT-001</code> covers every boundary |\\n16006:| Three mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive outcomes | Section 13.13 defines <code>safe_retry</code>, <code>explicit_rollback</code>, and <code>recoverable_parked_state</code>, their disjoint predicates, ambiguity rule, and required evidence. |\\n16007:| Every relevant inter-phase crash/restart boundary | The closed <code>CR-LAUNCH-*</code>, <code>CR-SYNC-*</code>, <code>CR-MAT-*</code>, <code>CR-GRACE-*</code>, <code>CR-FORCE-*</code>, <code>CR-FORK-*</code>, <code>CR-STOP-*</code>, <code>CR-RESUME-*</code>, and <code>CR-RESTORE-*</code> registry in Section 13.13 covers direct, task-board, movement, materialization, ownership, native-resume, and restore phases. |\\n16008:| No duplicate owner or silent fresh native session | Section 13.13 rejects two live/authoritative owners, unfenced continuation presented as safe recovery, new-session launch, fresh native handles/manager references, blank relabeling, and realm substitution. |\\n16017:| New logical/native identity; immutable source provider | Session Record 2 in Section 5.1 and Section 13.14 |\\n16018:| One adapter per environment and stripped authority | Sections 7.8, 13.14.1–13.14.2, and 16.4 |\\n===4.1 bootstrap section===\\n<code>ax pane</code>, headless automation, credential proof, transport/auth,\\nno AX ownership/authoritative-state transfer, and crash/security/conformance\\nnegative tests.\\n\\n### 4.1 Terminal backend interface\\n\\nEvery terminal backend MUST implement these semantic operations:\\n\\n| Operation | Required result |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| <code>create</code> | With <code>session_id</code> and caller-stable <code>bootstrap_operation_id</code>, create a durable pane/process whose only stable entry point is <code>ax pane SESSION_ID</code>. |\\n| <code>attach</code> | Connect an operator to the local terminal without changing ownership. |\\n| <code>status</code> | Report process presence, wrapper state, provider child state, and attachability. |\\n| <code>quiesce-input</code> | Stop new operator/provider input while retaining observation. |\\n| <code>wait-safe-boundary</code> | Prove the adapter-specific safe boundary or time out. |\\n| <code>request-stop</code> | Ask the provider to exit through a version-tested graceful path. |\\n| <code>terminate-stale</code> | Terminate a process only for explicit force recovery after preserving diagnostics. |\\n| <code>restore</code> | Recreate the wrapper after reboot and consult current ownership before any provider resume. |\\n\\nThe wrapper MUST validate configuration, load the logical session, synchronize\\nknown lease records when possible, and compare the local fencing token before\\nlaunching a provider. It MUST park safely when ownership is remote, ambiguous,\\nor unverified.\\n\\nTerminal creation and the wrapper's first child start are idempotent on\\n<code>(session_id, bootstrap_operation_id)</code>. The backend MUST durably bind\\nthat pair before child creation. An identical retry returns or reattaches to\\nthe one recorded wrapper/child; changing the operation for a session still in\\nthe bootstrap window is <code>idempotency_mismatch</code>. After a lost result,\\n<code>status</code> plus that binding MUST prove absence or identify the one\\nchild before any start retry. This contract never persists or replicates a PID\\nas identity.\\n\\n### 4.2 tmux backend\\n\\nOn macOS, Linux, and WSL2, the supported durable terminal backend is tmux. Every\\nmanaged pane MUST run:\\n\\n~~~shell\\nax pane <logical-session-id>\\n~~~\\n\\nIt MUST NOT run the raw provider command as the pane's stable command.\\n<code>tmux-resurrect</code> plus <code>tmux-continuum</code> MAY recreate local\\ntmux layout and invoke the wrapper after reboot. They MUST NOT be described or\\nused as cross-host migration.\\n\\nAX MUST use its dedicated <code>-S</code> server and MUST NOT discover or reuse\\nthe operator's default tmux server by ambient socket name. On macOS, server\\ncreation is credential-sensitive. A Background caller MUST NOT create a\\ncredential-dependent tmux server. A background CLI, SSH RPC process, daemon,\\nor restore worker MAY contact an already-running authenticated Aqua broker and\\nits attested AX tmux server; if neither exists it MUST return\\n<code>capability_unavailable</code> with typed realm/readiness details and MUST\\nNOT fall back to direct server creation.\\n\\n<code>launchctl managername</code> is a diagnostic hint only. Conformance\\nrequires a functional AX sentinel inside the exact tmux server plus a separate\\nprovider-auth smoke using the selected provider. Evidence MUST bind the exact\\ntmux server generation, provider build, and macOS version. Aqua alone and\\nsentinel-only evidence are insufficient. Any provider flow requiring GUI\\ninteraction, Keychain UI, a permission prompt, or other human action fails\\nclosed until that interaction succeeds in the verified GUI realm.\\n\\nAfter restore, the wrapper MUST:\\n\\n1. read the latest locally known lease;\\n2. attempt a mesh lease refresh without blocking forever;\\n3. resume locally only if the local host has the winning lease and the required\\n   materialization is valid;\\n4. offer remote attach/takeover when another host owns the session in an\\n===after-restore===\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 12666,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 3167,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","final_result":true}}}
